module gunbc.compiler_gate_workflow


// NO LANE THE REQUIRED CONTEXT READS RUNS ON A HOSTED RUNNER (operator ruling 2026-09-28). The
// lanes the `witnesses` aggregate reads -- `emit-build`, `floor` and `generated` -- run on the fleet,
// and so does the aggregate.
//
// THE HOSTED UNIT-TEST LANE WAS DELETED (operator ruling 2026-09-29). `rust-unit-tests` ran
// `cargo test --release -p v1-compiler --lib` on every pull request and merge_group as a
// non-blocking measurement, and its step's continue_on_error rendered every run green while the
// population was red on main -- a verdict nobody could read, bought with about a third of the
// hosted pool. That ruling said the tests return as a BLOCKING fleet lane once the population is
// green, never as a hosted measurement again, and on 2026-09-30 they did: `rust-unit-tests` is a
// blocking fleet lane read by the aggregate (see the unit-test section below).
//
// WHY THE HOSTED LANES LEFT THE MERGE PATH. The history below put them there because the fleet was
// the bottleneck. On 2026-09-28 the bottleneck was the hosted pool: it held at 20 running jobs with
// 66 waiting (GitHub Free's concurrency limit for hosted runners; the organization is on that
// plan), every pull-request run and every queued landing asked it for four jobs, and a landing's
// required context waited on that pool rather than on its own lanes. Receipts, re-read from the
// jobs API's created_at and started_at rather than trusted here: merge-queue runs 36478070174 and
// 36464765845, where every hosted job waited minutes for a slot -- the aggregate, whose one step
// runs in seconds, waited 9 and 21 -- while the fleet lanes of the same runs started within
// seconds. So `compiler` is deleted: its one step built `gunbc`, which the fleet prelude of both
// `floor` and `generated` already builds from the same tree (DESIGN section 2). The lint became a
// step on `generated` (compiler_gate_lint_step), and the aggregate moved to the fleet.
// compiler_gate_merge_path_runs_on_the_fleet refuses emission of a workflow in which a blocking
// lane or the aggregate runs hosted.
//
// `emit-build` BLOCKS, ON THE FLEET (operator sign-off 2026-10-02, given to the N7 manager as step
// 10 of the advisory program). It stayed hosted and announced while its only obstacle was the fleet
// claim its two compilations make on every pull request and merge_group; the operator agreed that
// cost, so its lane row is LaneBlocks and the job runs on the fleet runner with the fleet lanes'
// fork guard and skippable-on-fork arm. The roster did not grow: the job already existed, and only
// its standing and runner changed. It runs BESIDE `floor` rather than after it, so on the hosted
// runs before this change (whose emit-build wall was below the floor's) it adds no wall to a
// landing; the fleet wall is read from this lane's own runs, not restated here.
//
// SUPERSESSION CANCELS FLEET JOBS, AND THIS RULING DID NOT START THAT. The concurrency policy
// cancels a running pull-request run on the next push, and the history below justified that by the
// hosted runner. `floor` (2026-09-19) and `generated` (2026-09-27) joined this workflow on the fleet
// under the same policy, so the justification stopped covering the merge path when they did:
// gunbc.compile_permit_scope fleet_compile_cancellation_standing still reads NotSafe (producer
// absent), so a cancelled fleet compile may leak its ctrl-jobserver permits. This ruling moves one
// more cargo invocation, the lint, into that exposure and does not resolve it.
//
// HISTORY (2026-09-19), kept because its reasoning still prices the roster: THE REQUIRED CHECK WAS,
// FOR A TIME, "THE COMPILER BUILDS" -- AND NOTHING ELSE.
//
// This module emits .github/workflows/witnesses.yml in place of gunbc.witness_floor_workflow.
// The fold that workflow ran cost ~100 runner-minutes per PR push across two self-hosted jobs
// (build lane ~30 min, floor ~47 min) plus the heal job (~26 min), on a ~45-runner fleet that
// every session pushes to. Measured on run 35462055101: the floor's 4,185 claims executed in
// 53 seconds; the other ~42 minutes were corpus preparation (14.3 min strict-preparation of
// 3,197 modules), reach probes (11 min), parse/admission (~7 min) and receipt bookkeeping.
// The fleet queued for up to 49 minutes and the merge queue stalled behind it.
//
// The operator ruled that the required gate shrinks to building the compiler until the fold's
// preparation cost is fixed. So this workflow is one job, `witnesses` (the name the ruleset's
// required status check reads), that builds the `gunbc` binary and stops.
//
// IT RUNS ON A GITHUB-HOSTED RUNNER, which is what makes supersession lawful here. The fold keeps
// cancel-in-progress false because a killed job on a self-hosted host leaks its ctrl-jobserver
// permits (gunbc.compile_permit_scope, fleet_compile_cancellation_standing is NotSafe). A hosted
// runner joins no host jobserver, so a cancelled build leaks nothing, and a PR's superseded run is
// cancelled instead of holding a runner. merge_group and heal revalidation keep their immutable
// groups (witness_floor_concurrency_group), so they are never cancelled by a later push.
//
// DISSOLVE-ON: the fold's preparation cost is cut (resolved-module and reach-probe results
// reused across runs, or preparation scoped to a diff's closure) and its runtime fits the fleet;
// then witnesses.yml is emitted by gunbc.witness_floor_workflow again and this module is deleted.

